<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prajnaparamita</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Perfection of Wisdom sutras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Prajnaparamita, or Perfection of Wisdom, sutras are a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> Buddhist scriptures on the emptiness of all phenomena and the wisdom of the bodhisattva, the earliest of which date from around the first century BCE. Copies of the Prajnaparamita in Eight Thousand Lines, written on palm leaves and illustrated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature paintings</a>, are among the finest surviving products of the monasteries of the Pala Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prajnaparamita.jpg" alt="Prajnaparamita" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medicine_Buddha_painted_mandala_with_goddess_Prajnaparamita_in_center,_19th_century,_Rubin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Prajñāpāramitā means the Perfection of Wisdom, a perfected way of seeing the nature of reality, and also names a body of Mahāyāna scriptures that includes the Heart Sutra and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diamond-sutra">Diamond Sutra</a>. According to Edward Conze, the texts number about forty and were composed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> between about 100 BCE and 600 CE.</p><p>Western scholars have traditionally considered the Perfection of Wisdom in Eight Thousand Lines the earliest, probably put in writing in the 1st century BCE. That dating rests largely on the dates of its translations. The longer sutras, in 25,000 and 100,000 lines, are held by Nattier to be this text sliced up and filled with other material.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The sutras are generally associated with emptiness, the lack of essence, the illusory nature of things and the non-arising of all phenomena, and with the Madhyamaka thought of Nāgārjuna. Their central figure is the bodhisattva, defined in the eight-thousand-line text as one who trains in all phenomena without obstruction and knows them as they really are. A bodhisattva resolves to lead all beings to nirvana, not a single self. Paul Williams notes that the sutras also praise themselves at length.</p><p>A commentary lists eight main sutras by length, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diamond-sutra">Diamond Sutra</a> in 300 lines to the 100,000-line text. In the later phase of Indian Buddhism, from the 8th to the 11th century, short esoteric sutras with mantras were produced, among them the Heart Sutra.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 260 the Chinese monk Zhu Zixing travelled to Khotan and found a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> text of 25,000 lines. Some Khotanese Buddhists regarded it as heterodox and tried to prevent its removal, but it was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and translated by Mokṣala. Chinese translation began in the 2nd century, and translators included Kumārajīva in 408 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>, who began in 660 to translate a collection of 600 scrolls.</p><p>The sutras reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a> (742–796), brought by the scholars Jinamitra and Silendrabodhi. Tibetan scholars study them through the Abhisamayālaṅkāra. The Heart Sutra is recited by Buddhists throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and the Adhyardhaśatikā is widely recited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shingon-buddhism">Shingon Buddhism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prajnaparamita” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The bronze and black-stone sculpture of eastern India, which the Tibetan historian Taranatha associated with the artists Dhiman and his son Bitpalo, and the illustrated palm-leaf manuscripts of Buddhist texts such as the <b>Prajnaparamita</b>, provided models for the art of Nepal, Tibet, Myanmar and Java.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mahayana produced a large new literature of sutras presented as the Buddha’s words, among them the <b>Perfection of Wisdom sutras</b>, including the Diamond Sutra and the Heart Sutra, the Lotus Sutra and the Pure Land sutras.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
